    "status": "confirmed",
    "reasoning": "All 16 selected answers agree with their own captured Math Academy worked solutions.\nq-16230: Adding sin(2x)=2x−4x³/3+… and cos(x)=1−x²/2+… gives the stated first four terms.\nq-125912: Substituting 3x² into ln(1+u)=u−u²/2+u³/3−… gives 3x²−9x⁴/2+9x⁶.\nq-83429: Multiplication of the sine series by x² gives x³−x⁵/6+x⁷/120.\nq-47679: Continuity need not imply differentiability; a jump precludes differentiability; differentiability implies continuity. Thus II and III must hold.\nq-51072: Both one-sided limits at 1 equal 3, but f(1)=0. The function is discontinuous and therefore not differentiable there or throughout (−2,2). Only III holds.\nq-76490: The roots are a=arccos(1/6) and 2π−a. Their product is approximately 6.848, rounding to 6.8.\nq-14619: Completing squares gives (x−2)²+(y+2)²=13/2. The source contains an intermediate typo, writing 2·2x in the y group where 2·2y is required; its final center and radius are correct.\nq-15460: Completing squares gives (x+1)²+(y−5)²=81, hence center (−1,5) and radius 9.\nq-179490: P(A′∩B)=P(B)−P(A∩B)=11/20−1/4=3/10.\nq-178681: P(A′∪B)=1−P(A∩B′)=1−(0.4−0.1)=0.7.\nq-72733: The coefficient matrix has inverse [[1,−1],[1,−2]]. Multiplying by [1,2] gives the solution vector [−1,−3], exactly the representation in option 3.\nq-77805: The equation reduces to sin(x)=−√3/2. The roots in the specified domain are −π/3 and −2π/3, with product 2π²/9. The source incorrectly calls the second root's quadrant the second quadrant and later labels that root x₁; these intermediate errors do not change its final answer.\nq-120614: The equations require cosecant values 1, √2/2, and 1/5. Since real cosecant has absolute value at least 1, only II and III have no solutions.\nq-51432: The graph's slopes increase on (−3,−1) and (1,3), indicating concavity up.\nq-141937: The graph's slopes decrease on (−2,−1) and (−1,0). These intervals remain separate because the cusp at −1 prevents concavity down across their union as a single interval.\nq-12549: Inclusion-exclusion gives P(A∩B)=0.25+0.75−0.8=0.2, so the events are not mutually exclusive.",
